.Dd October 26, 2026
.Dt BSDINSTRUMENTS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bsdinstruments
.Nd DTrace-backed profiling templates with OpenTelemetry output
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -json
.Nm
.Cm watch
.Op Fl -broker
.Op Ar profile | Fl f Ar script.d
.Op Fl -param Ar key=value
.Op Fl -pid Ar n
.Op Fl -execname Ar name
.Op Fl -uid Ar n
.Op Fl -gid Ar n
.Op Fl -jail Ar jid
.Op Fl -where Ar predicate
.Op Fl -duration Ar seconds
.Op Fl -with-stack
.Op Fl -with-ustack
.Op Fl -format Ar text | json | otel | otlp-json | collapsed
.Op Fl -output Ar file
.Op Fl -rotate-size Ar size
.Op Fl -rotate-time Ar time
.Op Fl -rotate-keep Ar n
.Op Fl -endpoint Ar url
.Op Fl -spool Ar file
.Op Fl -spool-max Ar size
.Op Fl -bufsize Ar size
.Op Fl -switchrate Ar rate
.Nm
.Cm generate
.Op Ar profile | Fl f Ar script.d
.Op Ar rendering options ...
.Nm
.Cm probes
.Op Fl -broker
.Op Fl -provider Ar name
.Op Fl -regex Ar re
.Op Fl -pid Ar n
.Op Fl -json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
bundles a catalog of DTrace profiling templates covering system
tracing, file and network activity, allocations, scheduler and lock
contention analysis, and USDT-instrumented applications, and ships
the results as text, JSONL, collapsed stacks or OTLP JSON Lines to
standard output or a rotating file, or as OTLP/HTTP+JSON to an
OpenTelemetry collector.
.Pp
A profile is a plain
.Pa .d
file.
Profiles are loaded from three locations, in increasing precedence:
.Pa /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles
(base system),
.Pa /usr/local/share/bsdinstruments/profiles
(ports), and
.Pa ~/.bsdinstruments/profiles
(per-user).
When the same profile name appears in several locations the
higher-precedence file shadows the lower one and a warning is
printed to standard error.
.Ss Subcommands
.Bl -tag -width "generate"
.It Cm list
Print every profile the loader found.
With
.Fl -json ,
emit one JSONL record per profile.
.It Cm watch
Run a profile (or an explicit
.Fl f Ar script.d
file) via
.Xr dtrace 1 Ap s
libdtrace and stream its output through the selected exporter.
Requires root.
.It Cm generate
Render a profile to final D source without running it, applying the
same parameter, filter, stack, and duration processing as
.Cm watch .
Needs no privileges; useful for piping into
.Nm dtrace Fl s Ar - .
.It Cm probes
List DTrace probes available on this system, including USDT probes
of a running process when
.Fl -pid
is given.
.El
.Ss Profile markers
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li /* @bsdinstruments-predicate */
Replaced with the composed
.Fl -pid , -execname , -uid , -gid , -jail , -where
filter predicate.
.It Li /* @bsdinstruments-predicate-and */
Appended, with a leading
.Ql && ,
inside a profile's own predicate block.
.It Li /* @bsdinstruments-stack */ , /* @bsdinstruments-ustack */
Replaced with
.Fn stack /
.Fn ustack
actions when
.Fl -with-stack /
.Fl -with-ustack
is set.
.It Li ${name}
Substituted from
.Fl -param Ar name=value .
.El
.Ss Output formats
.Bl -tag -width "collapsed"
.It Cm text
Line-oriented dwatch-style output; libdtrace's formatted output goes
directly to standard output, in color on a terminal.
.It Cm json
One JSON object per output line, pipe-friendly for
.Xr jq 1 ,
Loki, or Vector.
.It Cm otel
Batch events as OTLP LogRecords POSTed to the collector's
.Pa /v1/logs
endpoint; DTrace aggregations become typed metrics on
.Pa /v1/metrics
(count/sum as monotonic Sums, min/max/avg as Gauges, quantize
variants as Histograms).
.It Cm otlp-json
The same OTLP requests written as JSON Lines, one request per line: the
format of the OpenTelemetry Collector's file exporter, readable by its
.Ql otlpjsonfile
receiver.
.It Cm collapsed
Folded stacks for
.Nm flamegraph.pl
or speedscope; requires
.Fl -with-stack
and/or
.Fl -with-ustack .
.El
.Ss Timestamps
Structured formats stamp each record with the probe's own wall-clock
time, not the time
.Nm
read it.
.Nm
enables a small clock program alongside the profile that prints
.Va walltimestamp
and
.Va timestamp
from the same firing at
.Dv BEGIN
and once a second; each record's high-resolution timestamp is moved
onto the wall clock by the latest of these anchors.
The time the record was read is kept as the observed time
.Pq Ql observedTimeUnixNano , or Va observed in JSON .
Profiles need no changes.
Text output carries no timestamps and does not run the clock program.
Records printed before the first anchor reaches
.Nm
fall back to the time they were read.
Cumulative metrics carry the time tracing started as their start time.
.Ss Output files
Every format except
.Cm otel
writes to standard output, or with
.Fl -output Ar file
to that file, appending to an existing one.
The file is checked before tracing starts, so a bad path fails at once.
Output written to a file is flushed and
.Xr fsync 2 Ns ed
at least once a second and at exit.
.Fl -rotate-size Ar size
.Pq with an optional k, m or g suffix
and
.Fl -rotate-time Ar time
.Pq with an optional s, m, h or d suffix
rotate the file when it would grow past the size or has been open that
long: the file becomes
.Ar file Ns .1 ,
older files shift up, and
.Fl -rotate-keep Ar n
.Pq default 7
rotated files are kept.
Records are never split across files.
Text output rotates at the next once-a-second check.
A write error, such as a full disk, stops the trace with exit status 1
and a message naming the file.
.Ss Collector outages
With
.Fl -format Cm otel ,
.Fl -spool Ar file
keeps batches the collector could not take after the retries: they are
appended to
.Ar file
as OTLP JSON Lines and resent, oldest first, once the collector accepts
a request again, either later in the same run or in a later run with
the same spool.
.Fl -spool-max Ar size
bounds the spool
.Pq default 64m ;
batches that do not fit are dropped and counted.
Batches the collector rejects as malformed are never spooled.
.Ss DTrace buffer tuning
.Fl -bufsize
sets the per-CPU trace buffer size (default 4m for text output, 16m
for structured output) and
.Fl -switchrate
the buffer drain cadence (default 50ms).
Increase both for extreme probe rates.
.Sh BROKER ACCESS
With
.Fl -broker ,
watch and probes request a delegated DTrace consumer from BSDTrace through
.Xr libtracecmp 3 .
The caller needs an authorized service connection and a client label explicitly
allowed by BSDTrace's configuration.
The tool does not add that authorization or fall back to local device access
when delegation fails.
Without this option, the existing local DTrace access path is used.
The broker must provision the required providers; delegated opening does not
load kernel modules or reopen provider devices.
Rendering with generate needs no tracing authority and does not accept this
option.
.Pp
Broker access does not make process attachment harmless: avoid probes --pid
and ptrace/ktrace attachment when profiling protected capability daemons.
Use global SDT and syscall profiles through the authorized tracing path.
.Sh LOADER AND SCRIPT PROFILES
The loader-libraries profile records requested library names and load outcomes
from the existing rtldtrace provider.
The loader-summary profile counts these requests by process, name and outcome.
Counts include requests satisfied by already-loaded objects; they do not
measure unique files or resident memory.
The loader must include DTrace support, and normal tracing permissions apply.
Protected-process tracing restrictions must not be relaxed for profiling.
Events are diagnostics and do not authorize executable content.
.Pp
The script-exec profile records attempted executable paths and exec outcomes.
For a shebang script, these can be correlated with the interpreter's library
loads by timestamp and PID.
Sourced scripts and input evaluated by an already-running interpreter do not
produce new exec events; use appropriate language probes for that activity.
Preserve and inspect collector errors and drop reports before drawing coverage
conclusions, and repeat performance measurements without instrumentation.
.Pp
The image-verification profile measures kernel embedded-signature checks:
operation counts, input and hashed byte counts, error results, and elapsed time.
Operation IDs are 0 for exec, 1 for O_VERIFY open, 2 for descriptor verification,
and 3 for executable-capable mmap.
The recognized field indicates a signature marker, not trusted policy authority.
Timing includes I/O and scheduling; it is not a cryptography-only CPU benchmark.
Unpaired completions indicate a check started before tracing was enabled.
These SDT probes add no profiling clock reads when disabled and record no paths,
descriptor numbers, public keys, policy contents or kernel addresses.
They require a kernel built with MAC_VERIEXEC_CAPIMAGE and SDT support.
Userland signing and bundle verification are outside
this profile's coverage.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The OTLP exporter honors the standard OpenTelemetry variables
.Ev OTEL_SERVICE_NAME ,
.Ev OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION ,
and
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT .
An explicit
.Fl -endpoint
flag takes precedence over
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT ,
which takes precedence over the built-in default
.Ql http://localhost:4318 .
Malformed values are reported and ignored as described in
.Xr libotelexport 3 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 when tracing or exporting fails, and 2 on a usage
error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Watch
.Xr kill 2
syscalls system-wide:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch kill
.Ed
.Pp
Trace TCP connects from nginx for 60 seconds as JSONL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch tcp-connect --execname nginx --duration 60 \e
    --format json | jq .
.Ed
.Pp
Ship syscall counts to an OpenTelemetry collector:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch syscall-counts --format otel \e
    --endpoint http://collector:4318 --duration 10
.Ed
.Pp
Record accept latency as OTLP JSON Lines, rotating daily and keeping a
week:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch accept-latency --format otlp-json \e
    --output /var/log/otel/accept.jsonl --rotate-time 1d
.Ed
.Pp
Ship to a collector, spooling through outages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch connect-latency --format otel \e
    --endpoint http://collector:4318 --spool /var/db/otel-spool.jsonl
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a flame graph of on-CPU stacks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bsdinstruments watch sched-on-cpu --with-stack --format collapsed \e
    --duration 10 | flamegraph.pl > out.svg
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr dwatch 1 ,
.Xr libotelexport 3 ,
.Xr hwstat 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in the ObservableBSD project and was ported from
Swift to C for inclusion in the base system.
